      "dom_id": "question-117549",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14226505/assets/ccdbd48b6cdf40205fe15a0b52553926aa4ba5e66e5a29bcf9ce6ae4164154a4.png)\n\nGiven that $r$, $s$ and $t$ in the above diagram are parallel, find $m∠v.$",
      "worked_solution": "Consider $∠2$ and $∠3$ in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14226505/assets/50d1e010a8279bf8f59a5e57b4ee4d4b744af7e431351bb17397e123aa79c59a.png)\n\nNote that $∠2$ and the angle whose measure is ${100}^{∘}$ are corresponding angles formed by the same transversal. Therefore, \n\n$$\nm∠2={100}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, note that $∠3$ and the angle whose measure is ${36}^{∘}$ are corresponding angles formed by the same transversal. Therefore, \n\n$$\nm∠3={36}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nLet's add these measures to our diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14226505/assets/ce9944a3fa28177bd0882fccd53d544159b6f041c570e7037cac7db31103130e.png)\n\nThe angles $∠v$, $∠2,$ and $∠3$ combined give a straight angle. So, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠v+m∠2+m∠3 & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠v+{100}^{∘}+{36}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠v+{136}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠v & ={44}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
